.TH JOIN 1 "March 2026" "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
join \- relational database operator
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B join
.RB [ \-a
.IR file_number
|
.B \-v
.IR file_number ]
.RB [ \-e
.IR string ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR list ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR char ]
.RB [ \-1
.IR field ]
.RB [ \-2
.IR field ]
.RB [ \-\-check-order | \-\-nocheck-order ]
.RB [ \-\-header ]
.RB [ \-i | \-\-ignore-case ]
.RB [ \-o auto ]
.RB [ \-z | \-\-zero-terminated ]
.IR file1
.IR file2
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B join
utility performs an "equality join" on the specified files and writes the result
to standard output.
.PP
A field must be specified for each file as the "join field" on which the files are
compared. The files must be sorted on the join field, using the collating sequence
specified by the appropriate locale.
.PP
By default, the fields are separated by blanks, with leading blanks ignored.
If the
.B \-t
option is specified, then the fields are separated by the specified character.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-a " " \fIfile_number\fP
In addition to the default output, produce a line for each unpairable line in file
.IR file_number ,
where
.I file_number
is 1 or 2.
As a BSD extension, if
.B \-a
is specified with no argument, unpairable lines from both files are printed.
.TP
.BR \-v " " \fIfile_number\fP
Instead of the default output, produce a line only for each unpairable line in file
.IR file_number ,
where
.I file_number
is 1 or 2.
.TP
.BR \-e " " \fIstring\fP
Replace empty output fields with
.IR string .
.TP
.BR \-o " " \fIlist\fP
Construct each output line according to the format specified by
.IR list .
Each element in
.I list
must be either the character '0' (representing the join field), or have the form
.IR file_number.field ,
where
.I file_number
is a file number and
.I field
is a decimal integer field number.
.TP
.BR \-t " " \fIchar\fP
Use character
.I char
as a field delimiter for both input and output.
.TP
.BR \-1 " " \fIfield\fP
Join on the
.IR field Nth
field of file 1.
.TP
.BR \-2 " " \fIfield\fP
Join on the
.IR field Nth
field of file 2.
.TP
.B \-\-check-order
Check that the input files are sorted. If they are not, exit with a non-zero status.
.TP
.B \-\-nocheck-order
Do not check that the input files are sorted. This is the default.
.TP
.B \-\-header
Treat the first line in each file as field headers, print them without trying to pair them,
and do not check their ordering.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-ignore-case
Ignore differences in case when comparing keys.
.TP
.BR \-z ", " \-\-zero-terminated
End lines with 0 byte (NUL), not newline.
.SH EXTENSIONS
GNU and BSD extensions are supported natively. When
.B \-a
is provided without arguments, this implementation falls back to BSD precedence
by printing unpaired lines from both files. GNU long-opts and zero-delimiter
semantics are fully adopted.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The
.B join
utility exits 0 on success, and >0 if an error occurs.
.SH SEE ALSO
sort(1), awk(1)
